---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Background, engineering philosophy, and personal story of Mando."
>
  <div class="max-w-[720px] mx-auto space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="space-y-3 pb-8 border-b border-zinc-200/80 dark:border-zinc-800/80">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-zinc-50">
        About Me
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 font-serif leading-relaxed">
        I am a software engineer focused on building robust, performant software systems that solve real problems with minimal accidental complexity.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Bio Sections with Editorial Typography -->
    <section class="article-prose space-y-6">
      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>
        I have spent over a decade working across the technology stack, from bare-metal Linux kernels and network daemons to high-throughput distributed microservices and modern frontend architectures.
      </p>
      <p>
        My core interest lies at the intersection of <strong>systems programming</strong>, <strong>data storage architectures</strong>, and <strong>developer ergonomics</strong>. I believe that understanding the underlying operating system, disk I/O characteristics, and network protocols makes one an exponentially better software engineer.
      </p>

      <h2>Engineering Philosophy</h2>
      <p>
        Over the years, shipping code to production has instilled a few guiding principles that inform every line of code I write:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Simplicity over cleverness:</strong> Code is read and debugged far more often than it is written. Boring, straightforward implementations with clear boundaries win over convoluted metaprogramming.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Observability is not an afterthought:</strong> If you cannot inspect the internal state of a running service using structured logs and clear metrics, you are running blind.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Respect the machine:</strong> Compute resources are finite. Writing efficient algorithms, avoiding memory bloat, and respecting cache hierarchies is good craftsmanship.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Minimal dependencies:</strong> Every third-party library is a liability in security, maintenance, and upgrade velocity. Vendor deliberately.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What I'm Currently Exploring</h2>
      <p>
        These days, I spend my spare time experimenting with:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Building high-performance storage engines and log-structured merge trees in Rust.</li>
        <li>Hardening my local homelab infrastructure with Debian, ZFS, WireGuard, and Docker.</li>
        <li>Static site generation and edge compute workflows using Astro and Cloudflare.</li>
        <li>Exploring distributed consensus protocols (Raft, Paxos) and write-ahead log designs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Get in Touch</h2>
      <p>
        I enjoy conversing with fellow engineers, exchanging notes on homelabs, and discussing distributed systems challenges.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can find my code on <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>, follow my thoughts on <a href="https://x.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Twitter / X</a>, or reach out directly via email at <code class="font-mono text-xs">mando [at] mando.dev</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
